WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.940
Long before your body can make new antibodies against a microbe, it is already

2
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:14.360
defending itself. Innate immunity is the set of defenses an organism has before it

3
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.900
meets a particular microbe.

4
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.220
Barriers keep many microbes out.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.620
For the ones that get in, ready-made receptors recognize whole classes of microbial

6
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.740
molecules, and signs of damage.

7
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:32.000
That recognition sets off a fast, coordinated response, inflammation, which brings

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.240
eating cells and blood proteins to the site.

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.490
In a mammal, resident cells such as macrophages carry pattern-recognition receptors.

10
00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.810
One, TLR4, recognizes lipopolysaccharide, a molecule from the outer membrane of many

11
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.410
bacteria. Sensing switches on inflammatory genes.

12
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:56.350
Mediators such as histamine widen small vessels and loosen their lining.

13
00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:01.230
Fluid and plasma proteins, complement among them, leak into the tissue.

14
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:05.070
Neutrophils squeeze out of the vessel, and monocytes follow.

15
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.430
Macrophages clear microbes and debris, and the tissue rebuilds.

16
00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:11.603
Barriers come first.

17
00:01:12.663 --> 00:01:14.943
Skin's outer layer is dead, tightly packed cells.

18
00:01:15.843 --> 00:01:19.223
In airways, mucus traps microbes, and cilia carry it out.

19
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:22.263
Stomach acid kills many swallowed microbes.

20
00:01:23.263 --> 00:01:28.603
Lining cells release antimicrobial peptides, and harmless resident microbes take the

21
00:01:28.603 --> 00:01:30.043
space a newcomer would need.

22
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:35.317
A microbe that gets through meets a phagocyte, a cell that engulfs particles.

23
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:39.797
Lysosomes fuse with the bubble that holds it and usually kill it.

24
00:01:40.457 --> 00:01:42.937
The evidence came from a transparent water flea.

25
00:01:43.797 --> 00:01:47.397
Elie Metchnikoff watched its blood cells swallow invading yeast spores.

26
00:01:48.297 --> 00:01:51.217
Where the cells destroyed the spores, the infection stopped.

27
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:53.597
Where spores grew, the flea died.

28
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.660
Complement is a set of proteins in blood plasma.

29
00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.920
When C3 is cut, its large piece, C3b, sticks to a microbe and marks it for eating.

30
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:10.300
The small pieces, C3a and, a step later, C5a, drift away and promote inflammation.

31
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.400
Later pieces can punch holes in susceptible microbes.

32
00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:21.303
Against viruses, interferons from infected cells put nearby cells into an antiviral

33
00:02:21.303 --> 00:02:25.683
state. Natural killer cells kill infected or abnormal cells.

34
00:02:26.543 --> 00:02:31.963
A cell that has lost a normal surface protein, MHC class I, removes a brake on them.

35
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:38.197
Flies make no antibodies, yet flies and plants each recognize microbes with their

36
00:02:38.197 --> 00:02:44.457
own receptors. Innate immunity does not build new receptors in each B or T cell, so

37
00:02:44.457 --> 00:02:46.337
it lacks their receptor-based memory.

38
00:02:47.197 --> 00:02:51.917
What it gives is speed: keep microbes out, recognize the ones that get in, and

39
00:02:51.917 --> 00:02:53.517
gather the defenses that clear them.
